{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1573638558,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDMwODI2MDA0MQ=="},"target":"0d71a38ccd647b1fee09eb102ea518c8bb9804eb10d6d6ea39e3c16986908de6","message":"It can be cumbersome to figure out what to tell `rerun` to rerun. The best approach that I can see is to use tags for relevant commits that need to be re-rerun (frequently). But tags can be a bit viral, so maybe we should have a convenience command that lists run records with the (abbrv) commits shas\n\nAlso, when we tackle https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3370 such a command would give us an abstraction layer to hide behind, when commit message content markup conventions.\n\nI think all needed functionality is already in `rerun --report`, we just need to expose it for querying, i.e.\n\n- allow convenient \"since commit 1\" query\n- (a result renderer) that is optimized for commit selection","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1573638789,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDMwODI2MjQ0NQ=="},"target":"0d71a38ccd647b1fee09eb102ea518c8bb9804eb10d6d6ea39e3c16986908de6","message":"It can be cumbersome to figure out what to tell `rerun` to rerun. The best approach that I can see is to use tags for relevant commits that need to be re-rerun (frequently). But tags can be a bit viral, so maybe we should have a convenience command that lists run records with the (abbrv) commits shas\n\nAlso, when we tackle https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3370 such a command would give us an abstraction layer to hide behind, when commit message content markup conventions.\n\nI think all needed functionality is already in `rerun --report`, we just need to expose it for querying, i.e.\n\n- allow convenient \"since commit 1\" query\n- (a result renderer) that is optimized for commit selection\n- (optionally) expose the recorded command for copy\u0026paste, such that we can easily pick a recorded command, tweak its parameters manually and then run the modified command. IOW be able to compose a full `datalad run` command line with inputs, outputs and all.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1574273415,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU1NjE4OTk0Ng==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3866#issuecomment-556189946"},"message":"\u003e It can be cumbersome to figure out what to tell `rerun` to rerun. The best approach that I can see is to use tags for relevant commits that need to be re-rerun (frequently). But tags can be a bit viral,\n\nConceptually this has overlap with gh-3371.\n\nYou could of course use branches instead of tags.  If you want them to be easily distinguishable from other branches, I'd think a prefix like \"run/\" would do.  Perhaps you don't like that the user would have to explicitly push the branches to share with others, or that branches are _not_ viral and others would have to refer to them qualified (ie., `\u003cremote\u003e/...`).  Anyway, just a comment; this is all very use case/user dependent.\n\n\u003e so maybe we should have a convenience command that lists run records with the (abbrv) commits shas\n\nI do think there is some value in users, especially those just learning git and datalad, realizing that they can do something like\n\n    git log --grep=RUNCMD --format=\"%h -- %s\"\n\nAnd I don't see that as particularly inconvenient.  It can of course include false positives (and as you mention next, we've discussed changing the particular markup), so it certainly seems useful to teach `rerun` more convenient ways to list the run commits it knows about.\n\n\u003e I think all needed functionality is already in `rerun --report`,\n\nYes, whether it's an actual rerun or `--report` or `--script` (the `rerun` code refers to these as \"handlers\"), the same collection of run records are used underneath.\n\n\u003e     * allow convenient \"since commit 1\" query\n\nHmm, I'm not sure I understand.  Are you referring to what is already possible with `--since=''`?\n\n\u003e     * (a result renderer) that is optimized for commit selection\n\nLike a line with an abbreviated hash and subject for each run commit?  Or do you have something else in mind?\n\n`--report` right now is only useful with `-f json{,_pp}` or a custom format.  It'd indeed be good to define a `custom_result_renderer` that recognizes records are coming from `--report` and displays something more useful.\n\n\u003e     * (optionally) expose the recorded command for copy\u0026paste, such that we can easily pick a recorded command, tweak its parameters manually and then run the modified command. IOW be able to compose a full `datalad run` command line with inputs, outputs and all.\n\nIIUC this is essentially the request from gh-3558.","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1574273415,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDMxMjQxMTAzOA=="},"target":"5d051a8f338a83c0f84421c330e9f6db7e1ae06f30b43d20b26b5b4806fe7a55","message":"\u003e It can be cumbersome to figure out what to tell `rerun` to rerun. The best approach that I can see is to use tags for relevant commits that need to be re-rerun (frequently). But tags can be a bit viral,\n\nConceptually this has overlap with gh-3371.\n\nYou could of course use branches instead of tags.  If you want them to be easily distinguishable from other branches, I'd think a prefix like \"run/\" would do.  Perhaps you don't like that the user would have to explicitly push the branches to share with others, or that branches are _not_ viral and others would have to refer to them qualified (ie., `\u003cremote\u003e/...`).  Anyway, just a comment; this is all very use case/user dependent.\n\n\u003e so maybe we should have a convenience command that lists run records with the (abbrv) commits shas\n\nI do think there is some value in users, especially those just learning git and datalad, realizing that they can do something like\n\n    git log --grep=RUNCMD --format=\"%h -- %s\"\n\nAnd I don't see that as particularly inconvenient.  It can of course include false positives (and as you mention next, we've discussed changing the particular markup), so it certainly seems useful to teach `rerun` more convenient ways to list the run commits it knows about.\n\n\u003e I think all needed functionality is already in `rerun --report`,\n\nYes, whether it's an actual rerun or `--report` or `--script` (the `rerun` code refers to these as \"handlers\"), the same collection of run records are used underneath.\n\n\u003e * allow convenient \"since commit 1\" query\n\nHmm, I'm not sure I understand.  Are you referring to what is already possible with `--since=''`?\n\n\u003e * (a result renderer) that is optimized for commit selection\n\nLike a line with an abbreviated hash and subject for each run commit?  Or do you have something else in mind?\n\n`--report` right now is only useful with `-f json{,_pp}` or a custom format.  It'd indeed be good to define a `custom_result_renderer` that recognizes records are coming from `--report` and displays something more useful.\n\n\u003e * (optionally) expose the recorded command for copy\u0026paste, such that we can easily pick a recorded command, tweak its parameters manually and then run the modified command. IOW be able to compose a full `datalad run` command line with inputs, outputs and all.\n\nIIUC this is essentially the request from gh-3558.","files":null}]}